public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
    List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
    backtrack(nums, result, new ArrayList<>(), 0);
    return result;
}

private void backtrack(int[] nums, List<List<Integer>> result, List<Integer> current, int begin) {
    if (begin == nums.length) {
        result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
        return;
    }
    for (int i = begin; i < nums.length; i++) {
        current.add(nums[i]);
        backtrack(nums, result, current, i + 1);
        current.remove(current.size() - 1);
    }
}
```
This implementation uses backtracking to generate all possible permutations of the input array. It uses a recursive approach by calling the backtrack method to generate permutations of the input array for each index in the range `begin` to `nums.length-1`. The backtrack method adds each permutation to the result list and then recursively generates permutations for the remaining indices.

